# `mcp.json` — connecting external tools & data (MCP)

## What this file is

**Model Context Protocol** is an open standard for connecting assistants to
external tools and data: databases, browsers, docs, ticket systems. You declare
**servers**; the harness acts as the **client** and calls them.

## Generic (vendor-neutral)

```jsonc
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "<name>": {
      "command": "<launch command>",   // local (stdio) server
      "args": ["..."],
      "env": { "API_KEY": "$ENV_VAR" }
    },
    "<remote-name>": {
      "url": "https://...",            // remote (http/sse) server
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer $ENV_VAR" }
    }
  }
}
```

Many tools (not just opencode) read an `mcpServers`-shaped file — that's the
portability win of a standard.

## opencode-specific — MCP config (copy this)

In opencode, MCP servers live under the `mcp` key of `opencode.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "playwright": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": ["npx", "-y", "@playwright/mcp@latest"],
      "enabled": true
    },
    "mongo": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": ["npx", "-y", "mongodb-mcp-server"],
      "environment": { "MDB_MCP_CONNECTION_STRING": "{env:MONGODB_URI}" },
      "enabled": false
    }
  }
}
```

- `type: "local"` → launched as a process (stdio). `--command` is an **array**.
- `type: "remote"` → connect to a URL.
- `enabled: false` keeps it declared but off until you need it.

## Safety note

An MCP server can read/write whatever you give it access to. Grant the
**narrowest** credentials that work, and prefer read-only where possible.
